<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palembang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palembang is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musi-river">Musi River</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was the core capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>, controlling shipping through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> straits. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedukan-bukit-inscription">Kedukan Bukit inscription</a> of 682 CE, found nearby, is the earliest known record of the Srivijayan state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palembang.jpg" alt="Palembang" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gaudi Renanda · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LRT_Palembang_Ampera_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Strait of Malacca had been dominated by Srivijaya, centred on <b>Palembang</b>, until the Cholas weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese Singhasari and Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anjukladang inscription of 937 rewards a village for repelling invaders from Malayu, that is Srivijaya, and in 990 Dharmawangsa launched a naval invasion to capture <b>Palembang</b>: Java's envoys of 992 told the Chinese court of continuous war, and Srivijaya had recovered by 1003.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ming restrictions on private overseas trade (the haijin ban) gave tribute missions a privileged position, and the court's own ships sailed to Ayutthaya in Siam, Malacca, <b>Palembang</b>, Java, Patani and Annam; letters and documents of these voyages survive in the Rekidai Hoan, the kingdom's diplomatic archive.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kedukan Bukit inscription of 682 CE, found near modern <b>Palembang</b> and written in Old Malay, among the earliest surviving examples of that language, records the ruler Dapunta Hyang Sri Jayanasa leading a military expedition and establishing his authority over the surrounding region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its earliest known stone inscription, found by the Dutchman C. J. Batenburg in 1920 at Kedukan Bukit on the Tatang River near <b>Palembang</b> in South Sumatra, is written in the Pallava variety of the Grantha alphabet and dated 1 May 683.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1615 Dutch and English traders had opened factories there to buy pepper grown in the upriver highlands, and the court used this wealth to rival Johor and <b>Palembang</b> for control of the trade of eastern Sumatra.</blockquote><a href="/state/jambi-sultanate">Jambi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
